1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:07,172
On Mars, the sunset is blue. This is not an
artist's guess. Rovers have photographed it.

2
00:00:07,750 --> 00:00:11,439
The same Sun sets on every
world in the solar system.

3
00:00:11,439 --> 00:00:17,508
But no two sunsets look alike. Here they
are, one by one, through the same lens.

4
00:00:20,500 --> 00:00:22,272
Start with the one we know.

5
00:00:22,272 --> 00:00:29,137
From Earth, the Sun is half a degree wide, and
takes about two minutes to slip below the horizon.

6
00:00:29,750 --> 00:00:33,389
Air molecules scatter blue
light far more than red.

7
00:00:33,389 --> 00:00:45,878
At noon, that scattered blue fills the sky. At sunset, the light crosses so much air
that most of the blue is scattered away before it reaches you. What is left is red.

8
00:00:48,500 --> 00:00:48,858
Mars.

9
00:00:48,858 --> 00:01:04,696
Half as far again from the Sun, so the disc is about two thirds the size. The air is thin, less than a
hundredth of Earth's, but it is full of fine, rust-coloured dust. By day, that dust turns the sky butterscotch.

10
00:01:05,500 --> 00:01:15,180
At sunset, the grains are just the right size to scatter blue light
forward, in a tight cone around the Sun. So the colours are reversed:

11
00:01:15,180 --> 00:01:18,082
a tawny sky, and a cold blue glow.

12
00:01:20,750 --> 00:01:22,636
Mercury has almost no air.

13
00:01:22,636 --> 00:01:34,595
So there is no colour at all. The sky is black, even at noon, and the Sun, up
to three times wider than ours, drops behind the rock with nothing to soften it.

14
00:01:35,250 --> 00:01:36,611
It takes its time.

15
00:01:36,611 --> 00:01:54,499
One day here lasts a hundred and seventy-six Earth days. And near its closest approach to the Sun, Mercury's orbital
motion briefly outruns its spin. From some places, the Sun sets, stops, rises again, and then sets a second time.

16
00:01:57,250 --> 00:01:59,581
Venus has the opposite problem.

17
00:01:59,581 --> 00:02:15,420
Too much air. From the surface, under a permanent deck of sulphuric acid cloud, you never see the Sun's
disc at all. Daylight is a dim orange overcast, and sunset is just that overcast, slowly going dark.

18
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:16,843
Very slowly.

19
00:02:16,843 --> 00:02:30,544
Venus spins backwards, and one turn takes longer than its year. From one sunrise to the next
is a hundred and seventeen Earth days. And if you could see the Sun, it would rise in the west.

20
00:02:33,250 --> 00:02:36,541
The giant planets have
no ground to stand on.

21
00:02:36,541 --> 00:02:54,375
So float at the cloud tops of Jupiter. Out here, the Sun is a fifth as wide as it is from Earth, and twenty-seven times
fainter. But Jupiter spins once in under ten hours. So that tiny disc drops through the horizon in about ten seconds.

22
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:07,170
At Saturn, the Sun is a tenth the width. But it is not the Sun you would be
looking at. The rings rise out of the haze and arch across the whole sky:

23
00:03:07,170 --> 00:03:11,014
countless pieces of ice,
catching the last light.

24
00:03:13,750 --> 00:03:18,115
Uranus lies on its side, and one
orbit takes eighty-four years.

25
00:03:18,115 --> 00:03:40,164
Near a pole, the Sun circles the sky without setting for about forty-two of them, spiralling lower with every turn. When it finally touches the horizon, the
tiny disc is gone within a couple of days. But the twilight can linger for years. Farther out still, at Neptune, the Sun shrinks to a thirtieth the width.

26
00:03:42,750 --> 00:03:49,806
Titan, Saturn's largest moon, has air thicker
than Earth's, and an orange haze of organic smog.

27
00:03:49,806 --> 00:04:00,853
In two thousand and five, the Huygens probe landed here, among pebbles
of water ice, in light about a thousand times dimmer than an Earth day.

28
00:04:01,500 --> 00:04:05,351
The haze lets red light
through, and blocks the blue.

29
00:04:05,351 --> 00:04:18,202
So over a sea of liquid methane, the Sun never shows a clean edge. It sinks into a
dull amber glow. And the dusk drags on, because one day on Titan lasts sixteen of ours.

30
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:27,442
And Pluto. Forty times farther out than Earth.
The Sun is no longer a disc. It is a star:

31
00:04:27,442 --> 00:04:34,311
a single point, yet still about two hundred
and fifty times brighter than our full Moon.

32
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:40,045
Noon on Pluto is about as bright as
Earth a few minutes after sunset.

33
00:04:40,045 --> 00:04:49,026
NASA calls that moment Pluto time. And when New Horizons
looked back, it saw layer upon layer of haze, glowing blue.

34
00:04:51,750 --> 00:05:04,200
Beyond our own Sun, it gets stranger. Kepler-16b circles two stars. It is a gas giant.
But from a moon of it, if it has one, you would watch two suns go down together:

35
00:05:04,200 --> 00:05:06,937
one orange, one small and red.

36
00:05:09,750 --> 00:05:14,270
Every one of these views is a
reconstruction, built from measurements.

37
00:05:14,270 --> 00:05:20,129
Robots have photographed a few of them. No
person has ever stood under any of them.

38
00:05:21,250 --> 00:05:26,017
There is only one sunset that human
eyes have watched from the ground.

39
00:05:26,017 --> 00:05:30,807
This one. It happens every evening.
It is worth looking up for.

